Chapter 1 — Mathematical Preliminaries

1.1 Notation

Let \Sigma=\{0,1\}. For x\in\Sigma^n, let |x|=n. For a codec i\in[k]=\{1,\dots,k\}, let E_i:\Sigma^n\to\Sigma^* and D_i:\Sigma^*\to\Sigma^n satisfy D_i(E_i(x))=x. Write s_i(x)=|E_i(x)|. Let b=\lceil\log_2 k\rceil be the index width in bits, and c the marker width in bits.

For a selector \varphi:\Sigma^n\to\{0,1,\dots,k\}, where 0 denotes raw, let L_\varphi(x) be the length of the frame produced on x. Let L_A(x)=\min\!\bigl(n,\,b+c+\min_i s_i(x)\bigr) with the compressed branch taken only on strict inequality.

1.2 The link‑layer contract

Definition 1.1 (Contract). A link‑layer contract is a triple

\mathcal{K}=(n,\ \mathsf{len},\ \mathsf{mode})

where:

· n is the fixed packet length, known a priori to both encoder and decoder;
· \mathsf{len} specifies how the decoder learns the transmitted frame length (e.g., a physical‑layer length field);
· \mathsf{mode} specifies how the decoder distinguishes raw from compressed frames.

A selector is contractually non‑expanding under \mathcal{K} if for every x\in\Sigma^n, the frame F(x) satisfies |F(x)|\le n and the decoder G, given \mathcal{K}, recovers x.

Definition 1.2 (Resilient contract). A resilient contract is a quadruple

\mathcal{K}_\varepsilon=(n,\ \mathsf{len},\ \mathsf{mode},\ \varepsilon)

where \varepsilon is an error model specifying admissible corruption events. A selector is resiliently non‑expanding if it is contractually non‑expanding under \mathcal{K}_\varepsilon and, for every frame corrupted by an event in \varepsilon, the decoder returns either the correct x or \bot, except with probability at most \delta_{\mathrm{undet}}, where \delta_{\mathrm{undet}} is the undetected‑error probability of the integrity mechanism.

