Chapter 3 — Resilient Framing

3.1 The frame format

Definition 3.1 (Resilient frame). For x\in\Sigma^n,

F(x)=\begin{cases}
x & \text{if raw is chosen},\\[2pt]
\langle i\rangle_b\ \cdot\ \mathsf{prot}(m=1)\ \cdot\ E_i(x) & \text{if codec }i\text{ is chosen},
\end{cases}

where \mathsf{prot}(m) is a c-bit CRC of the one‑bit mode m concatenated with the b-bit index \langle i\rangle_b. The CRC covers both the mode and the index, so a corruption of either is detected with the CRC's undetected‑error probability.

Definition 3.2 (Admissibility). Codec i is admissible on x if

b+c+s_i(x)<n.

If no codec is admissible, the selector transmits raw. The strict inequality ensures the marker fits inside the saving.

3.2 CRC undetected‑error probability

We use the standard CRC model: a c-bit CRC detects all single, double, and triple bit errors, and all burst errors of length \le c, for frames whose length does not exceed 2^c-1 bits. For random errors, the undetected‑error probability is bounded by 2^{-c}.

Lemma 3.3. Under E1 (payload bit flips only), a c-bit CRC over the protected header (m,\langle i\rangle_b) detects any corruption of the header with certainty if the header length plus CRC is at most 2^c-1, and detects any random corruption with probability at least 1-2^{-c}.

Proof. Standard CRC detection properties; the frame length bound follows from the generator polynomial degree.

