Chapter 5 — SAT‑SELECT‑B and Regret

5.1 Algorithm

```
Algorithm 2: SAT-SELECT-B (resilient contract)
Input : x ∈ Σ^n, φ, codecs, b, c
1. i ← φ(x)
2. if i = 0: return x
3. s_i ← |E_i(x)|
4. if b + c + s_i < n:
5.     return ⟨i⟩_b · prot(1) · E_i(x)
6. else: return x
```

5.2 Theorem 5.1 (Resilient non‑expansion and losslessness of B)

The statements of Theorems 4.1 and 4.2 hold with s_{\varphi(x)} in place of s_{i^*}, and with the additional raw branch i=0 returning x.

Proof. Identical case analysis to Theorems 4.1 and 4.2. \square

5.3 Theorem 5.2 (Regret)

Let L_B(x)=|F_B(x)|. Then L_B(x)\ge L_A(x) for all x, with equality iff \varphi(x) is an action that achieves the minimum in Theorem 4.4, i.e., either \varphi(x)=0 when raw is optimal, or \varphi(x)=i where b+c+s_i(x)=\min_j(b+c+s_j(x))<n.

Proof. L_A is the minimum over admissible actions by Theorem 4.4; L_B chooses one action from that set. Equality holds exactly when the chosen action attains the minimum. \square

Correction 5.3. The equality condition is framed‑length minimality, not "smallest raw compressed payload." A codec with the smallest s_i may have a larger b+c+s_i than another codec if the index or marker costs differ — although in the current framing b and c are constant across codecs, so the distinction is immaterial here. It becomes material if per‑codec marker lengths are introduced.

5.4 Theorem 5.4 (Cost advantage on correct raw prediction)

If \varphi(x)=0, then T_B(x)=T_\varphi(n). This is operationally meaningful only if J_{\mathrm{dec},B} is small relative to J_{\mathrm{POCK+}}; the theorem is conditional on the measured cost model.

