Chapter 6 — Query Complexity and the Limits of Exact Selection

6.1 The black‑box query model

Definition 6.1 (Black‑box query model). There are k candidate encoders. The only way to learn s_i(x)=|E_i(x)| is to query encoder i on x. Each query costs T_{E_i}(n). A selector is exact if it returns an action attaining the minimum in Theorem 4.4 for every x.

6.2 Proposition 6.2 (Exhaustive query lower bound)

Any exact deterministic selector in the black‑box query model must, in the worst case, query all k encoders.

Proof. Suppose a selector queries fewer than k encoders and leaves encoder j unqueried on some input x. Construct an alternative input x' that agrees with x on all queried encoders but on which encoder j has s_j(x')=\min_i s_i(x')-1 and is admissible. The selector cannot distinguish x from x', so it cannot return the correct action on both. Hence every exact selector queries all k encoders in the worst case. \square

Remark 6.3. This is a query‑count lower bound, not a computational‑complexity lower bound. It leaves open the possibility that SAT‑SELECT‑B exploits structure in telemetry data to avoid querying encoders whose output lengths are predictable from features. That possibility is an empirical hypothesis (H3), not a theorem.

6.3 Remark on the MTS connection

The online version of the problem — choosing codecs over a stream without knowing future packets — is a metrical task system with k+1 states (raw plus k codecs). Borodin, Linial, and Saks showed that every deterministic online algorithm for an n-state MTS has competitive ratio at least 2n-1, independent of the metric. Specialising gives a lower bound of 2(k+1)-1=2k+1 for the codec‑selection MTS.

Important qualification. This bound applies to the general MTS. It does not follow automatically that the restricted family of telemetry tasks generated by a particular mission's packet distribution is sufficiently general to attain the bound. The MTS remark is therefore a point of reference, not a theorem about SAT‑SELECT. The primary lower bound is Proposition 6.2.

