Chapter 8 — The Break‑Even Frontier

8.1 Objectives

For a distribution \mathcal{D} over \Sigma^n and selector \varphi, define

R(\varphi)=\mathbb{E}[L_\varphi(x)],\qquad
C(\varphi)=\mathbb{E}[T_\varphi(x)],\qquad
J(\varphi)=\mathbb{E}[J_\varphi(x)].

The energy is decomposed as

J = J_{\mathrm{compute}} + J_{\mathrm{transmit}} + J_{\mathrm{memory}} + J_{\mathrm{other}},

and compute and transmit are reported separately. Raw transmission has R=n, C=0, J_{\mathrm{compute}}=0, but J_{\mathrm{transmit}}>0; assigning raw J=0 was an error in R3.

8.2 Pareto nondomination

Definition 8.1 (Pareto‑optimal selector). A selector \varphi is Pareto‑optimal in a family \Phi if no \varphi'\in\Phi satisfies R(\varphi')\le R(\varphi), C(\varphi')\le C(\varphi), and J(\varphi')\le J(\varphi), with at least one strict inequality.

Theorem 8.2 (Frontier structure).

1. Raw is Pareto‑optimal: no selector can have R<n with C=0 or J_{\mathrm{compute}}=0, since compression requires computation and produces length <n only by encoding.
2. SAT‑SELECT‑A has the minimum R in the class of selectors using the same contract and candidate set (Theorem 4.4), and is Pareto‑optimal if no other selector achieves the same R at lower C or J.
3. Every fixed method i is a special case of B with \varphi\equiv i and therefore lies on or above the frontier defined by A, raw, and the best learned B.
4. A non‑trivial frontier exists on a distribution \mathcal{D} iff the nondominated set contains at least one selector other than raw and A.

Proof. (1) is the definition of raw. (2) follows from Theorem 4.4 and Definition 8.1. (3) is Theorem 5.2 applied to constants. (4) is the definition of nondomination. \square

Remark 8.3. R3's condition "R<R_{\text{fixed}} and C<C_A simultaneously" is not the definition of Pareto optimality and is withdrawn. A selector can be Pareto‑optimal without beating two named baselines in two named objectives.

8.3 Empirical grounding

The frontier must be computed on packet‑level data with full framing overhead. The published mission‑level tables — POCKET+, LZ4, Gzip, Bzip2 on Venus Express, Rosetta, GOCE, Gaia, and Herschel — are aggregate file‑level or stream‑level measurements and do not by themselves establish packet‑level performance on 100–200‑octet independent packets. They serve as an a priori plausibility check for H4 (that fixed‑method regimes exist), not as a substitute for the packet‑level experiment.

For reference, the published mission‑level figures show:

· Venus Express: POCKET+ 19.30% at 3.84 s; LZ4 33.70% at 1.91 s; Gzip 26.06%; Bzip2 19.84%.
· Rosetta: POCKET+ 13.89% at 1.45 s; LZ4 22.67% at 0.71 s; Gzip 17.73%; Bzip2 12.00%.
· GOCE: POCKET+ 39.79% at 34.91 s; LZ4 64.93% at 9.81 s; Gzip 55.82%; Bzip2 52.58%.
· Gaia: POCKET+ 27.36% at 8.14 s; LZ4 45.66% at 1.79 s; Gzip 38.26%; Bzip2 30.75%.
· Herschel: POCKET+ 14.52% at 13.01 s; LZ4 28.84% at 5.79 s; Gzip 23.14%; Bzip2 14.91%.

LZ4 compresses better than POCKET+ on all five datasets in these tables, at lower runtime. The first draft's assumption that POCKET+ is the reference fixed method is empirically unsupported on this evidence.

