Appendix B — Full Proofs and Technical Lemmas

B.1 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Probabilistic error‑aware losslessness)

We restate and prove in full, since R3's version was withdrawn.

Theorem 4.2. Let the frame be as in Def. 3.1 with a c-bit CRC \mathsf{prot} over the header h=(m,\langle i\rangle_b) of length 1+b. Under E1 (payload bit flips, length metadata intact), the decoder returns x or \bot except with probability at most 2^{-c}. Under E2 (length metadata corruption), the decoder reads the header independently of the length field; if the header CRC checks, (m,i) is correct except with probability at most 2^{-c}, and if it fails, the decoder returns \bot.

Proof.

Case E1, compressed branch. The decoder reads b+1+c header bits, verifies the CRC. By Lemma 3.3, if the header is corrupted, the CRC detects it except with probability 2^{-c}. If the CRC fails, return \bot. If it checks, (m,i)=(1,i) correctly except with probability 2^{-c}; the decoder then applies D_i to the payload. A corrupted payload either (i) decodes to a wrong string (undetected) or (ii) fails the frame checksum. The residual undetected probability is bounded by the checksum's own undetected‑error probability, which we include in the same 2^{-c} term by choosing a payload checksum of the same width c.

Case E1, raw branch. The length is n and no header is present. The decoder applies the packet checksum; corrupted payload is detected except with probability 2^{-c}, and if detected returns \bot; otherwise the frame is raw.

Case E2, compressed branch. The corrupted length field \tilde\ell\neq|F(x)| does not participate in the decision: the decoder reads the protected header from the first b+1+c bits and checks it. If the CRC checks, the decoder knows |F(x)|=b+c+s_i(x) from the header‑derived index and codec metadata, not from \tilde\ell; it truncates accordingly. If the CRC fails, return \bot.

Case E2, raw branch. Raw frames carry no protected header. The decoder attempts to read the first b+1+c bits as a header; since a random raw packet's first bits are not a valid protected header except with probability 2^{-c}, the decoder concludes raw except with probability 2^{-c}. This is the cost of distinguishing raw from compressed without a marker, and it is the reason the contract must specify \mathsf{mode} precisely (Def. 1.1).

The union over all cases gives the stated bound. \square

Remark B.1. This theorem is probabilistic, not deterministic. It is valid for random bit‑flip models with independent flip probability, and for the E2 model under which the length field is corrupted independently of the header. It does not cover adversarial corruption that respects the CRC's undetected patterns; the CRC detects all single/double/triple errors and bursts up to c bits under Lemma 3.3, but not all arbitrary patterns.

B.2 Lemma B.2 (CRC detection, restated)

Lemma 3.3. A c-bit CRC with generator polynomial g(z) of degree c detects every single, double, and triple bit error in a frame of length \le 2^c-1 bits, every burst of length \le c, and every error polynomial e(z) that is not divisible by g(z). For random independent bit flips with flip probability p\in(0,1) and e(z) uniform over nonzero polynomials of degree <N, the undetected‑error probability satisfies \Pr[g(z)\mid e(z)]\le 2^{-c} for c prime (for the standard primitive polynomials used in telemetry CRCs).

Proof. Standard CRC theory: the error polynomial is e(z), the received codeword is c(z)+e(z), and detection fails iff g(z)\mid e(z). For a burst of length \ell\le c, e(z)=z^j\,b(z) with \deg b<\ell, so \deg e< c and g cannot divide e. For random e, the divisibility probability is 2^{-c} for primitive g by the uniformity of residues modulo g. \square

Remark B.3. For the E2 case (length field corruption), the header must be protected. This is why the frame layout places the CRC over (m,\langle i\rangle_b), not over the length field (which is outside the frame). The contract's \mathsf{len} specification is a physical‑layer guarantee, and if it is not guaranteed, the resilient frame cannot in general detect the corresponding mis‑decode. This scoping is why the R3 claim of "error‑aware losslessness" was withdrawn.

B.3 Proof of Proposition 6.2 (Black‑box query lower bound)

Proposition 6.2. Any exact deterministic selector in the black‑box query model must, in the worst case, query all k encoders.

Proof. Fix x. Suppose an exact selector S queries the set Q(x)\subseteq[k] on input x and returns action a(x). If Q(x)\neq[k], choose j\notin Q(x). We construct x' that agrees with x on every queried encoder's output length and differs only on encoder j, such that j is strictly the best action on x'.

Because the query model only reveals |E_i(x)| for i\in Q(x), the selector's transcript on x and x' is identical (both reveal the same lengths for queried encoders). Hence S(x')=S(x)=a(x).

Choose x' such that s_j(x')<s_i(x')-b-c for all i\in Q(x) and s_j(x')<n-b-c (admissible). Such x' exists if the codec family is rich enough; the black‑box model places no restriction on codec behaviour other than losslessness on the input it is applied to, so an input x' with the required length profile is admissible. Then action j attains L_A(x')=b+c+s_j(x')<b+c+s_{a(x)}(x') if a(x)\neq j, and if a(x)=0 then L_A(x')<n. Either way, a(x)\neq j is not exact on x'.

Therefore an exact selector queries every encoder in Q(x) for every x, and in the worst case this is k queries. \square

Remark B.4. The proof requires that the codec family is rich enough to realise any length profile on some input. This is a property of the black‑box model: we assume each E_i can produce any length in [0,n] on some input. Real codecs may not be so rich, and that is precisely the room for SAT‑SELECT‑B to exploit structure. The proposition is therefore about the worst‑case over codec families, not about any particular family.

B.4 Derivation of the McAllester bound (Theorem 7.1)

We state the McAllester form used in the main text and derive its numerical evaluation.

Theorem 7.1. Let \mathcal{H} be a hypothesis class, P a prior, Q a posterior, and \ell(\varphi,x)\in[0,n] a bounded loss. For m i.i.d. samples S=(x^{(1)},\dots,x^{(m)})\sim\mathcal{D}^m and any \delta\in(0,1), with probability at least 1-\delta over S, for every posterior Q,

\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}}\mathbb{E}_{Q}[\ell] \;\le\; \mathbb{E}_{S}\mathbb{E}_{Q}[\ell] + n\sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{KL}(Q\|P)+\log(2m/\delta)}{2m}}.

Proof sketch. This is McAllester's bound for bounded losses, in the form

\mathbb{E}_\mathcal{D}\mathbb{E}_Q[\ell] \le \mathbb{E}_S\mathbb{E}_Q[\ell] + \sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{KL}(Q\|P)+\log(2m/\delta)}{2m}}\cdot(\text{range}),

with range =n and the standard union bound over m sample sizes (which contributes \log m inside the log term). The interchange of \mathbb{E}_Q and the sample average is justified by Fubini. \square

Numerical evaluation. Fix n=200 bits, m=10^4, \delta=0.05, and suppose the posterior is concentrated on a single hypothesis so that \mathrm{KL}(Q\|P) is \approx\ln(m) for a uniform prior over m hypotheses. Then

n\sqrt{\frac{\ln m + \ln(2m/\delta)}{2m}}
=200\sqrt{\frac{\ln 10^4+\ln(4\cdot 10^4)}{2\cdot 10^4}}
\approx 200\sqrt{\frac{9.21+11.90}{20000}}
\approx 200\cdot 0.0325\approx 6.5\text{ bits}.

This is non‑vacuous (below n=200) provided the empirical regret is below \approx 6.5 bits. For a depth‑5 decision tree over k=4 codecs, the KL term over a uniform prior on tree structures can be much larger, and the bound may become vacuous. The thesis's Corollary 7.2 tests this numerically at the actual m and model, and reports the vacuous case honestly.

B.5 Lemma B.5 (Regret nonnegativity, restated from Theorem 5.2)

Lemma B.5. For any selector \varphi, \ell(\varphi,x)=L_\varphi(x)-L_A(x)\ge 0.

Proof. Theorem 4.4 states L_A(x)=\min(n,b+c+\min_i s_i(x)) is the minimum admissible framed length over the candidate set. L_\varphi(x) is the framed length of some admissible action (or raw), so L_\varphi(x)\ge L_A(x). \square

This justifies the loss interval [0,n] in Theorem 7.1 and replaces R3's looser [-n,n].

