Appendix D — Worked Counterexamples

D.1 Proposition 2.3 (length‑only fragility under E2) — full computation

Take n=128, b=2, c=0 (length‑only). Codec 1 produces E_1(x) of length 120 bits. Frame: \langle 1\rangle_2\cdot E_1(x), length 2+120=122<128. Admissibility test: 2+0+120=122<128, yes.

Under E2, the physical‑layer length field is corrupted from 122 to 128. Decoder rule for length‑only: if length =n=128, return frame as raw. The decoder returns the 128‑bit string consisting of \langle 1\rangle_2\cdot E_1(x) plus 6 padding bits (physical‑layer padding). This is not x and not \bot. Silent mis‑decode.

Under the resilient frame with c=16, the decoder reads the first 3+16=19 bits, checks CRC. If corrupted, returns \bot. If not corrupted, it reads (m,i)=(1,1), then truncates the payload using the codec‑internal length. The corrupted external length field is ignored. Correct decode or \bot.

D.2 Proposition 2.1 (flag impossibility) — minimal example

n=1, x=0. Flag f=0 (raw). Frame: 00, length 2>1. \square.

D.3 Theorem 4.4 (candidate‑set optimality) — worked numbers

k=3, b=2, c=16, n=200. Suppose:

· s_1(x)=100 → admissible, frame length 118.
· s_2(x)=90 → admissible, frame length 108.
· s_3(x)=185 → not admissible (frame length would be 203>200).
· raw → 200.

Then L_A(x)=\min(200,108,203)=108. SAT‑SELECT‑A chooses codec 2. Candidate‑set optimality: no selector using the same contract and \{E_1,E_2,E_3\} produces length <108.

A selector using a different codec outside the candidate set could produce shorter; the theorem is silent on that.

D.4 Theorem 5.2 (regret) — worked numbers

Suppose on input x' the same codecs give s_1=90, s_2=100, s_3=185. L_A(x')=108 (codec 1). If \varphi(x')=2, L_B(x')=b+c+s_2=118, regret 10 bits. If \varphi(x')=3, s_3 not admissible, raw branch, L_B(x')=200, regret 92 bits. If \varphi(x')=0, L_B(x')=200, regret 92 bits (since codec 1 is admissible).

The regret is framed‑length regret, not compression ratio regret.

