Appendix E — Statistical Protocol Details

E.1 Power analysis

For a two‑sided two‑sample test of the difference in mean regret per packet, \varepsilon=L_B-L_A, with common standard deviation \sigma (estimated from a pilot split of the mission data),

m \;\ge\; \frac{2\,(z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta})^2\,\sigma^2}{\varepsilon^2}.

At \alpha=0.05, \beta=0.2 (power 0.8), z_{0.975}+z_{0.8}=1.96+0.84=2.80, so

m \;\ge\; \frac{2\cdot 7.84\cdot\sigma^2}{\varepsilon^2}=\frac{15.68\,\sigma^2}{\varepsilon^2}.

The thesis reports the pilot \sigma and the resulting m per mission. If a mission's packet count is below m, the thesis reports the detected minimum effect size at power 0.8 for that mission, rather than failing to declare significance.

E.2 Bootstrap confidence intervals for R, C, J

For each selector \varphi, compute the per‑packet triples (L_\varphi(x^{(j)}), T_\varphi(x^{(j)}), J_\varphi(x^{(j)})). Use the block bootstrap (block size = an entire mission segment) to preserve temporal dependence. Report 95 % percentile intervals with B=10^4 resamples. Declare a selector \varphi Pareto‑dominating \varphi' only when the intervals are disjoint in the appropriate direction.

E.3 Temporal split

Split the mission data by time block (e.g., first 70 % of the mission by timestamp for training, last 30 % for held‑out), not by random packet. This prevents adjacent packets from leaking into training and held‑out sets, which would overstate generalisation on temporal data. The split unit is reported alongside the regret estimate.

E.4 Vacuity test (Corollary 7.2)

Compute the RHS of Theorem 7.1 numerically at actual m, n, \mathrm{KL}(Q\|P). If RHS > n, report "vacuous at this sample size" and use bootstrap intervals only. This is the honest outcome and is reported as such, not suppressed.

E.5 Error injection protocol

For H2 (error‑aware losslessness):

· Bit‑flip rates p\in\{10^{-5}, 10^{-4}, 10^{-3}\} applied independently to each bit of the frame.
· Erasure bursts of length \{1,2,4,8\} placed at uniformly random offsets.
· For each injected corruption, record the decoder output; classify as {correct, \bot, silent mis‑decode}.
· Report the empirical rate of silent mis‑decodes and its 95 % Wilson interval. The theoretical bound 2^{-c} predicts rate \le 1.5\times10^{-5} for c=16; the empirical rate must be consistent with this.

