I.10 Black‑Box Query Model (Ch. 6, Prop. 6.2)

```
  SELECTOR  ──query i=1──▶  ┌──────────┐  ──▶ |E_1(x)|
                            │  E_1     │
                            └──────────┘
              ──query i=2──▶  ┌──────────┐  ──▶ |E_2(x)|
                            │  E_2     │
                            └──────────┘
              ──query i=j──▶  ┌──────────┐  ──▶ |E_j(x)|
                            │  E_j     │
                            └──────────┘
              ──query i=k──▶  ┌──────────┐  ──▶ |E_k(x)|
                            │  E_k     │
                            └──────────┘

  CLAIM (Prop. 6.2):  if selector skips query for index j,
                      adversary constructs x' where j is best,
                      selector's transcript on x and x' identical,
                      so selector picks wrong action on one of them.
                      ⟹ exact selector must query all k.

  CONSEQUENCE:  T_A(n) = Σᵢ T_{Eᵢ}(n)  in the WORST CASE.
                SAT-SELECT-B escapes this ONLY by exploiting
                STRUCTURE in telemetry packets (a hypothesis,
                not a theorem).
