I.14 Pareto Frontier in (R, C, J) Space (Ch. 8)

```
  R (expected length, bits) — lower is better
    ▲
  n │  ● RAW                      ← R = n, C = 0, J_transmit > 0
    │   \
    │    \        ● FIXED_i       ← R < n, C > 0, J > 0
    │     \      /
    │      \    /
    │       \  /
    │        ● A                  ← R minimal, C maximal
    │
    └──────────────────────────────▶  C (decision cost)

  THREE-DIMENSIONAL PICTURE:
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  J                                  │
  │  ▲                                  │
  │  │      ● RAW (C=0, J_transmit>0)   │
  │  │     /                            │
  │  │    ● FIXED_i                     │
  │  │   /                              │
  │  │  ● B(learned)  ← the interesting │
  │  │ /                 region: low C, │
  │  │/                  low R, low J   │
  │  ●────────────────────────────────▶ │
  │ A (minimal R, max C)      C          │
  └─────────────────────────────────────┘

  PARETO-OPTIMAL (Def. 8.1):
     φ is Pareto-optimal  iff  no φ' has
     R(φ') ≤ R(φ),  C(φ') ≤ C(φ),  J(φ') ≤ J(φ)
     with at least one strict.

  NON-TRIVIAL FRONTIER: the nondominated set contains
                        at least one point other than RAW and A.
